{"schemaVersion":1,"systemCode":"BSI:ron-Cyrl:Latn:1958","dependencies":[],"metadata":{"authority_id":"bsi","id":"1958","language":"iso-639-2:ron","source_script":"Cyrl","destination_script":"Latn","name":"BS 2979:1958 Transliteration of Cyrillic and Greek characters - Table E, Transliteration of Rumanian Cyrillic (Alfabetul Cirilic)","url":"https://www.bsigroup.com","creation_date":"1958","confirmation_date":"2000-05","description":"BS 2979:1958, Table E: transliteration of Rumanian Cyrillic (Alfabetul Cirilic),\n    the Cyrillic alphabet used for Romanian until 1860. For transliteration of\n    Moldavian in the modern orthography, the table's companion note directs the\n    reader to Table C (ISO/R9) in Section 2.","notes":["The table's footnote marks variant renderings encountered in latinised Rumanian that make for ambiguity in retransliteration and should therefore be avoided in documents for export: No. 6 ie (after vowels); 8 z; 23 ch; 24 o; 29 a-breve alternatives; 30 i and a-circumflex alternatives; 32 e; 36 e and ia; 37 u-breve; 40 t; 41 i. This map applies the primary renderings as printed.","K is romanized c, and ch before e and i; this map implements the context rule for the letters e, i and i-diaeresis following k, and renders c elsewhere. Dzhe (row 43, shaped like ts with a descender) is romanized g, the table conditioning the rendering on position before e and i.","Yer is romanized a with breve; yery as a-circumflex plus i; yat as ea; the little yus as e with circumflex and the big yus as a with circumflex. The soft sign is u with breve, omitted when final; this map renders it u with breve in all positions and does not implement the final-omission rule, which requires word-boundary handling the note leaves to the user.","The semiyus (row 42) is printed with the rendering i-circumflex and the parenthetical n; this map renders the letter as the two-letter reading in.","The digraph uk (o plus u, with monograph-uk variants) is romanized u with breve, the 